{"term":"adultero","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"adultery","concept":"extramarital_lover","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":25,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"An adulterer; a married person who has sexual relations outside of marriage, violating the conjugal bond.","english_term":"adulterer","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Registry lacks a dedicated adulterer concept; extramarital_lover is closest available. Adultero denotes the married party who commits the betrayal, not the outside lover."}
{"term":"fedeltà","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"marital_fidelity","concept":"monogamy","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":0,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Fidelity; the virtue of sexual and romantic faithfulness to one's partner, especially within marriage, carrying strong moral and religious weight in Italian culture.","english_term":"fidelity","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Mapped to monogamy as closest registry concept; fedeltà names the virtue of faithfulness rather than the structural fact of having one partner. Central to the honor-shame system as the feminine counterpart to male sexual freedom."}
{"term":"infedeltà","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"infidelity","concept":"love_affair","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":20,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":98,"recognition_pct_peak":98,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Infidelity; the state or act of being sexually unfaithful to one's partner, especially within marriage.","english_term":"infidelity","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"No dedicated infidelity concept in registry; love_affair is closest available. Infedeltà can describe a single act or a pattern without necessarily implying an ongoing romantic liaison."}
{"term":"bastardo","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"illegitimacy_insult","concept":"bastard_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":65,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Bastard; used as a general insult for a contemptible person, or literally to denote a child born outside of marriage.","english_term":"bastard","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Retains its original literal meaning (illegitimate birth) more strongly in Italian than in English, where it has largely shifted to pure insult; in Italian both senses remain fully active."}
{"term":"figlio illegittimo","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"illegitimate_birth","concept":"bastard_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":15,"technical":30,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Illegitimate child; the formal or legal term for a child born outside of marriage, carrying heavy social stigma in the traditional Italian honor-shame framework.","english_term":"illegitimate child","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Registry has no dedicated illegitimate-birth-status concept; bastard_insult is closest available. Now dated in everyday speech since Italian law abolished the legal distinction in 1975."}
{"term":"figlio di nessuno","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"illegitimate_birth","concept":"bastard_insult","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":25,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Child of nobody'; a poetic or euphemistic expression for an illegitimate child, emphasizing social abandonment and the absence of a recognized father.","english_term":"nobody's child","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Captures the social shame dimension more vividly than the legal term; the 'nobody' framing evokes paternal denial and community exclusion rather than mere legal status."}
{"term":"donnaiolo","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"womanizer","concept":"womanizer","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":30,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A womanizer; a man who habitually pursues and seduces many women, often described with a tone of grudging admiration rather than pure condemnation.","english_term":"womanizer","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Can be used admiringly in Italian male culture; the double standard encoded here — male promiscuity as prowess, female promiscuity as shame — is central to the honor-shame system."}
{"term":"cascamorto","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"persistent_suitor","concept":"suitor","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":15,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A theatrical admirer who makes exaggerated, dramatically lovestruck displays of romantic interest; literally 'fallen dead', evoking collapsing at the sight of a beautiful person.","english_term":"lovesick suitor","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The theatrical-performative element — publicly dying of desire — is specifically Italian/Mediterranean and has no precise English equivalent. Can be mocking or affectionate. Mapped to suitor as closest registry concept."}
{"term":"rovina famiglie","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"homewrecker","concept":"debaucher","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":45,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":75,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Family wrecker'; a woman who seduces married men and thereby destroys their family units.","english_term":"homewrecker","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Always applied to women in traditional usage; encodes the honor-shame expectation that women are responsible for protecting family sanctity. The married man is rarely blamed equivalently. Debaucher is closest registry concept."}
{"term":"amante","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"extramarital_affair","concept":"extramarital_lover","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":15,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Lover; a person with whom one is having a romantic or sexual relationship, typically implying an ongoing and often illicit or extramarital liaison.","english_term":"lover","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Italian amante is more commonly used and less literary-sounding than English 'lover'; its surface neutrality masks strong honor-shame implications when the relationship is extramarital."}
{"term":"mantenuta","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"kept_woman","concept":"kept_woman","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":40,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":84,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A kept woman; a woman financially supported by a man outside of marriage in exchange for sexual availability and companionship.","english_term":"kept woman","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Always feminine in Italian; carries social stigma in the honor-shame framework while simultaneously implying a pragmatic arrangement. Slightly dated as a term but still widely recognized."}
{"term":"donna di facili costumi","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"sexual_permissiveness_pejorative","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":35,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Woman of easy habits/customs'; a classic Italian euphemism for a sexually permissive woman, imputing moral looseness through behavioral indirection rather than direct accusation.","english_term":"woman of easy virtue","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The circumlocution signals shame through indirection — naming the social behavior ('easy customs') rather than the body or act. Common across generations; somewhat more formal than ragazza facile."}
{"term":"donna leggera","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"sexual_permissiveness_pejorative","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":30,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":79,"recognition_pct_peak":84,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Light woman'; a dated euphemism for a sexually permissive or promiscuous woman, using lightness as a metaphor for moral looseness.","english_term":"woman of loose morals","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"'Leggerezza' (lightness) has a long literary history in Italian as a metaphor for moral unreliability, especially in women. Slightly more dated than donna di facili costumi."}
{"term":"ragazza facile","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"slut_shame_woman","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":42,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Easy girl'; a common Italian expression for a young woman perceived as sexually permissive or easily persuaded into sex.","english_term":"easy girl","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"More direct than donna leggera; primarily applied to younger women. The Italian 'facile' parallels English 'easy' almost exactly in usage and connotation in this context."}
{"term":"ci va con tutti","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"promiscuity","concept":"sexual_promiscuity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":52,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'She goes with everyone'; a verbal idiom describing a woman as sexually promiscuous, stating she has sex with any willing man.","english_term":"she sleeps with everyone","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Phrase rather than single lexeme; highly transparent in meaning. The 'tutti' (everyone) signals indiscriminate sexuality, which is the core of the shame implication. Almost always applied to women."}
{"term":"gira","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"promiscuity","concept":"sexual_promiscuity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":58,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":73,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'She goes around / she rotates'; crude shorthand verb implying a woman is sexually promiscuous.","english_term":"she gets around","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Context-dependent: gira alone simply means 'she turns/goes around', but in the relevant register it functions as a shorthand accusation of promiscuity. More common in spoken than written Italian; lower recognition reflects its colloquial rather than literary status."}
{"term":"puttanella","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"slut_shame","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":65,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":87,"recognition_pct_peak":87,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Diminutive of puttana (whore); 'little slut' or 'little whore' — used harshly as an insult or sometimes with mock-affectionate irony.","english_term":"little slut","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The diminutive suffix -ella softens relative to puttana but the word remains strongly pejorative. In some regional uses it carries a cruel ironic tenderness, e.g. as a teasing address between women who know each other well."}
{"term":"di dubbia moralità","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"sexual_permissiveness_pejorative","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":20,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":83,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Of dubious morality'; a euphemistic adjective phrase used to impute sexual promiscuity or moral looseness without naming the behavior explicitly.","english_term":"of dubious morality","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The most indirect and formally polite way to impute sexual impropriety in Italian; favored in official, journalistic, or older-generation speech. Slightly higher technical score because the formal register borrows from legal/ethical discourse."}
{"term":"femminiello","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"third_gender_roles","subcategory":"neapolitan_third_gender","concept":"neapolitan_third_gender","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"subculture","crude":20,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":62,"peak_year":null,"regional":"IT-NA","definition":"A person assigned male at birth who lives in a feminine role within traditional Neapolitan society, occupying a recognized third-gender position with specific cultural and ritual functions.","english_term":null,"english_translation_quality":"none","notes":"Not equivalent to Western transgender identity; femminielli traditionally hold auspicious roles in Neapolitan popular religion and folk ritual — presence at a birth is considered to bring luck. The category is specific to Campania's cultural context and predates modern LGBTQ frameworks by centuries. Crude score low because within Naples the role commands respect."}
{"term":"femminiella","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"third_gender_roles","subcategory":"neapolitan_third_gender","concept":"neapolitan_third_gender","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"subculture","crude":20,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":48,"recognition_pct_peak":55,"peak_year":null,"regional":"IT-NA","definition":"Feminine grammatical variant of femminiello; refers to the same Neapolitan third-gender social role, sometimes treated as interchangeable with femminiello and sometimes as a slightly more distanced external descriptor.","english_term":null,"english_translation_quality":"none","notes":"Some Neapolitan community members prefer femminiello as the self-referential form; femminiella can feel more like an outsider's label. Recognition outside Naples is lower than for the base form. The -a ending imposes a binary feminine grammatical gender that arguably sits uneasily with the concept's third-gender status."}
{"term":"minchia","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":90,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":"IT-SI","definition":"Sicilian dialect for penis; an extremely common expletive across Sicily and southern Italy that functions as an all-purpose intensifier analogous to English 'fuck' or 'damn'.","english_term":"cock","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Though rooted in Sicilian dialect, minchia is now near-universally recognized throughout Italy via television and internal migration. As an exclamation it expresses surprise, admiration, or dismay without necessarily invoking its anatomical meaning. One of the most versatile expletives in the Italian dialect spectrum."}
{"term":"mona","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"vagina","concept":"vulva","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":85,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":"IT-VE","definition":"Venetian dialect for the vulva or vagina; also used as a general insult meaning fool or idiot.","english_term":"cunt","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"In Veneto, mona is so entrenched in everyday speech that it loses much shock value in regional context — comparable to how 'cunt' is less severe in parts of the UK than in the US. The dual use (genitalia + idiot) parallels English 'cunt' and 'twat' structurally."}
{"term":"belin","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":82,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":63,"peak_year":null,"regional":"IT-GE","definition":"Ligurian/Genoese dialect for penis; widely used in Genoa and Liguria as a general expletive, intensifier, and marker of regional identity.","english_term":"cock","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Strongly identified with Genoese regional identity; functions grammatically as both noun and exclamation. National recognition has grown via media but the word remains predominantly associated with the Ligurian dialect zone."}
{"term":"casso","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":83,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":63,"recognition_pct_peak":68,"peak_year":null,"regional":"IT-VE","definition":"Venetian and northeastern Italian dialect for penis; commonly deployed as an expletive alongside or interchangeably with mona.","english_term":"cock","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Venetian dialect equivalent of standard Italian cazzo; the dialect form gives it regional color. Pairs naturally with mona in Venetian expletive speech (casso e mona). Lower national recognition than minchia because it lacks the same media diffusion."}
{"term":"picio","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":35,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":52,"recognition_pct_peak":57,"peak_year":null,"regional":"IT-VE","definition":"Venetian dialect diminutive for penis, primarily used for a child's anatomy or as a mild, affectionate diminutive; also the standard Venetian adjective meaning 'small'.","english_term":"little willy","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Low crude score reflects its primary register as a non-adult term; the word's semantic ambiguity (it means 'small' in Venetian generally) reduces its taboo force. Occasionally deployed pejoratively against an adult male as an emasculating diminutive."}
